{% set emojiBase64 = "" %}

<p>Making your own website is simpler than it might seem and it’s a nice alternative to putting your work up for the world to see on increasingly monetised social media platforms. What’s more, having your own website grants you an exceptional amount of control over it. You can change the style as you see fit, not just within the confines of what’s imposed by FaceBook or Twitter or Tumblr. You can make your text</p>
<p style="font-size:3em;margin:0;text-align:center;color:red">very large and red</p>
<p>or <span style="font-size:.7em;color:blue">very small and blue</span>; you can use <span style="font-variant:small-caps;text-decoration:wavy underline">Small Caps With a Wavy Underline</span>, you can use custom emojis <img alt="small emoji" id="img-emoji" style="width:1em" src="data:image/png;base64,{{ emojiBase64 }}"> that invert colours when you hover over them etc. etc. In short, you can break any and all stylistic guidelines, written or unspoken, and there’s no-one to stop you.</p>

<style> #img-emoji { transition: filter linear .5s; &:hover, &:focus { filter: invert(1) } } </style>

<p>Contrary to what you might think, you can also host a website for free. In the course of this article, I’ll try to teach you &mdash; the, presumably, uninitiated reader &mdash; the basics of creating a webpage. Note that this is targeting <em>recreational, unprofessional</em> webpage creation. This is not a comprehensive guide to web design, not even close. It’s more of “learn to write HTML like it’s 2005” kind of article.</p>

<p>So, let’s begin with a brief explanation of <em>how</em> webpages work and then an introduction into writing them, piece by piece.</p>

<h1>What is a website, anyway?</h1>

<p>Let’s say you open your browser and go to a website, say, <a href="https://example.com/">https://example.com</a>. Your browser will look at that URL and understand that it needs to contact the server called “example.com” and ask for its <em>index</em>, which is sort of like the homepage. When it does so, the server responds by communicating that everything went properly and sends an HTML file containing the code of the webpage, which will contain references to CSS files which define the styling to apply (all text should be in Arial, tables should have a 1 pixel wide border etc.) and Javascript files, little programs that say how the webpage is supposed to behave (what should happen when I click on this button, what happens when the user minimises the browser etc).</p>

<p>Like a JPEG file or a PNG file encode how an image is supposed to look, HTML encodes the contents of a webpage. Unlike JPEG and PNG, however, HTML files are meant to be readable and writeable by humans, which means that you can open them in a text editor and you should be able to understand them with no issue. HTML stands for <dfn>HyperText Markup Language</dfn>. The word “markup” is significant; unlike regular programming languages, which tend to very strict about their structure, markup languages treat anything that doesn’t have its special characters as regular text.</p>

<p>You can try this out for yourself. Open a text editor &mdash; I don’t mean a word processor like Microsoft Word or LibreOffice Writer, but an actual text editor like Notepad or Sublime Text. Then copy the code below in it:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
Hello, world!

This is my webpage.
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>Save this file as {{ "file.html" | inlineCode }} and double-click it (or go to your browser and choose File &gt; Open File). You should see something similar to what’s in the Preview section above. There you go, you have just made an (admittedly very simple) webpage! You will have noticed that the webpage you’ve just made ignored the whitespace and put the text on one line. That’s because HTML makes no assumptions. You have to tell it <em>what</em> you want it to display. Otherwise, it will interpret anything you give it in the exact same way: as text with un-important whitespace. Writing HTML will be the subject of the following section.</p>

<h1 id="writing-html">Writing HTML</h1>

<p id="def-paragraph">How do we structure our webpage, then? The most important thing to know when writing HTML are <dfn>tags</dfn>. Tags can be <dfn>paired</dfn>, like the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/p">{{ "<p>" | inlineCode }} tag,</a> which is used to mark up a paragraph. In code, we just write the opening tag {{ "<p>" | inlineCode }} before the beginning of the paragraph and a closing tag {{ "</p>" | inlineCode }} after the end. These tags ({{ "<p>" | inlineCode }} and {{ "</p>" | inlineCode }}) create a {{ "p" | inlineCode }} <dfn>element</dfn>. An <dfn>element</dfn> is a unit in the abstract model of our web page that the browser creates. Tags are just the sequence of characters that tell the browser displaying our web page: “This is where the element’s content begins and this is where it ends.” Let’s change our webpage slightly:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>Hello, world!</p>

<p>This is my webpage.</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p id="def-br">The {{ "<p>" | inlineCode }} tags tell the browser to create two paragraph elements, one after the other. Some tags, like the line-break tag, don’t need to be paired. All we need to do is to write <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/br">{{ "<br>" | inlineCode }}</a> where we want to break the paragraph in two lines. This tag is <dfn>unpaired</dfn>, so there’s no need for us to close it with a corresponding {{ "</br>" | inlineCode }} tag.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>Hello, world<br>and all who inhabit it!</p>

<p>This is my webpage.</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

{% sidenote %}
<p>Historically, unpaired tags used to be written with a slash before the {{ ">" | inlineCode }} character, so the line break tag would be written as {{ "<br/>" | inlineCode }} or {{ "<br />" | inlineCode }}. This style is still valid, but isn’t favoured anymore.</p>
{% endsidenote %}

<p id="def-h1">We can give our document a sense of structure with the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/Heading_Elements">{{ "<h1>" | inlineCode }}, {{ "<h2>" | inlineCode }}, {{ "<h3>" | inlineCode }}, {{ "<h4>" | inlineCode }}, {{ "<h5>" | inlineCode }} and {{ "<h6>" | inlineCode }}{# </h1> #} elements.</a> {{ "<h1>" | inlineCode }}{# </h1> #} creates a level 1 heading, {{ "<h2>" | inlineCode }}{# </h2> #} creates a level 2 heading, and so on.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<h1>My document</h1>
<p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet.</p>

<h2>Section 1</h2>
<p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet.</p>

<h2>Section 2</h2>
<p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet.</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p id="def-hr">To introduce a thematic break, we can use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/hr">{{ "<hr>" | inlineCode }} (horizontal rule) element.</a> Much like {{ "<br>" | inlineCode }} (<a href="#def-br">defined above</a>), {{ "<hr>" | inlineCode }} is unpaired; don’t write a closing {{ "</hr>" | inlineCode }} tag after it. {{ "<hr>" | inlineCode }} will make a line:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>Upon waking in her room, Joanna felt a strange feeling of emptiness. Her husband was dead. There was nothing else she had to do.</p>

<hr>

<p>Police Commissioner Konstanz had an uneasy night.</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p id="def-blockquote">For quoting things, the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/blockquote">{{ "<blockquote>" | inlineCode }} element</a> is quite handy. Generally, you should put the quoted content inside of paragraph elements too.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>A wise man once said:</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>“Because bread tastes better than key.”</p>
    <p>– Wise Man.</p>
</blockquote>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>Throughout the article, we’ll sometimes use <dfn>comments</dfn> in the code to explain things. Comments start with {{ "<!--" | inlineCode }} and end with {{ "-->" | inlineCode }}. They aren’t visible to the user. They’re essentially little notes left by programmers for themselves or for other programmers.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
This is text <!-- and this is a comment --> and this is more text.
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<h2>Attributes and links</h2>

<p id="def-anchor">Another thing we need to talk about before we get into more complicated examples are <dfn>attributes</dfn>. Sometimes, we need to add additional information to a element. A good example are links: in HTML, these are called <dfn>anchors</dfn> and are created using the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/a">{{ "<a>" | inlineCode }} element.</a> An anchor needs two things to be functional: a text to display and a URL to bring the user to when clicked. The text is simply what’s written between the opening {{ "<a>" | inlineCode }} and the closing {{ "</a>" | inlineCode }}. The URL is indicated using the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/a#href">{{ "href" | inlineCode }} attribute:</a></p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>
    <a href="https://youtube.com">This is a link to YouTube</a>.
</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>As you can see, an attribute is written as {{ 'NAME="VALUE"' | inlineCode }}. <strong>Don’t forget the quotes.</strong> The code <em>can</em> work without them, but it might break in some circumstances. Also, specifically when it comes to the {{ "href" | inlineCode }} attribute, you can’t just write {{ 'href="youtube.com"' | inlineCode }}. Your browser needs the <em>whole</em> URL, otherwise it will assume that {{ "youtube.com" | inlineCode }} is the name of a file somewhere else on the website and clicking the anchor will take you there.</p>

<p>Anchors can also take another attribute: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/a#target">{{ "target" | inlineCode }}</a>. When used with the value of {{ 'target="_blank"' | inlineCode }}, the link will open the URL in a new tab:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>Clicking on <a href="https://example.com" target="_blank">this link</a> will open https://example.com in a new tab. When you wish to return to this article, simply close the tab.</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>Links can also be used to move around a document, not just from one document to the next. For this, we need to specify an <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Global_attributes/id">{{ "id" | inlineCode }} attribute</a> on the element we want to jump to and link to it with an {{ "<a>" | inlineCode }} element. So, if we want to create a link to a heading, for example, we’ll write it as {{ '<h1 id="my-heading">' | inlineCode }}{# </h1> #}. Then, if we want to link to it somewhere else in the document, we write {{ '<a href="#my-heading">' | inlineCode }}{# </a> #}. Notice that <strong>the {{ "id" | inlineCode }} attribute doesn’t contain a hashtag, but the {{ "href" | inlineCode }} attribute does.</strong> This is perfectly logical: {{ 'id="my-heading' | inlineCode }} means “the ID of this element is {{ "my-heading" | inlineCode }},” while {{ 'href="#my-heading"' | inlineCode }} means “this link will jump to an element on this same page with the ID (#) of {{ "my-heading" | inlineCode }}.”</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<h1 id="main-title">Main Title</h1>

<p>Some text. Some text. Some text. Some text. Some text. Some text. Some text. Some text. Some text. Some text. Some text.</p>

<a href="#main-title">Jump back to main title</a>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>Some attributes can also be specified without a value. For example, the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Global_attributes/hidden">{{ "hidden" | inlineCode }} attribute</a> can be used on <em>any</em> element and will prevent it from being shown to the user. This is not that useful, but it can come in handy in specific circumstances. Here’s an example of how to use it:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>This is my first paragraph. The user is meant to see it.</p>
<p hidden>This is my second paragraph. The user is NOT meant to see it.</p>
<p>This is my third paragraph. This one should be seen.</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>{{ "hidden" | inlineCode }} and {{ "id" | inlineCode }} are <dfn>global attributes</dfn>, which means they can be used with <em>any</em> elmenent. Other attributes, like {{ "align" | inlineCode }}, can only be used on specific elements. {{ "align" | inlineCode }} controls the alignment of the element. Possible values are {{ "left" | inlineCode }} (default), {{ "right" | inlineCode }} and {{ "center" | inlineCode }}.</p>

<p id="def-div">If you want to align multiple paragraphs to the centre, you could add {{ 'align="center"' | inlineCode }} to each one. An alternative is to put them inside of a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/div">{{ "<div>" | inlineCode }} element</a> with {{ 'align="center"' | inlineCode }}. {{ "<div>" | inlineCode }} (content division) is used to group content together for styling purposes.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<h1 align="center">My Amazing Title</h1>

<p>My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text. My amazing text.</p>

<div align="center">
    <p>We have nothing to fear... but fear itself.</p>
    <p>– FDR</p>
</div>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<h2>Typesetting elements</h2>

<p id="def-em">Now we have gone over the most important aspects of HTML syntax. We have only seen a couple of elements so far though, so let’s take a look at a few others. Firstly, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/em">{{ "<em>" | inlineCode }}</a>{# I just want it to not be in italics in my editor</em> #}, short for “emphasis,” which is used to display important text. By default, it just makes the text render in italics. Here is an example:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>This is one of the <em>worst</em> marketing campaigns I had ever seen!</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p id="def-strong">Similarly, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/strong">{{ "<strong>" | inlineCode }}</a>{# </strong> #} puts strong emphasis on what’s inside. Let’s see an example again:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>
    AI-isms aren’t just <em>annoying</em>, they’re <strong>insufferable</strong>—some might even say <strong><em>unforgivable</em></strong>.
</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p id="def-u-s-mark-sup-sub">Among the other font-related elements are <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/u">{{ "<u>" | inlineCode }}</a>{# </u> #}, used for underlining text, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/s">{{ "<s>" | inlineCode }}</a>{# </s> #}, which strikes the text out, and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/mark">{{ "<mark>" | inlineCode }}</a>, which highlights the text in yellow. Furthermore, there’s <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/sup">{{ "<sup>" | inlineCode }}</a> for superscript text (like powers: 2<sup>2</sup>) and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/sub">{{ "<sub>" | inlineCode }}</a> for subscript (like in chemical formulae: H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>). Here’s an example that puts them all together:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>
    Your suggestion regarding CO<sub>2</sub> emissions is <s>weclo</s>
    <u>welcome</u> and we look forward to our next collaboration.
    We will contact you around <mark>October 19<sup>th</sup></mark>.
    Would that be OK with you?
</p>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<h2>More typesetting with the {{ "<font>" | inlineCode }} element</h2>

<p id="def-font">The <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/font">{{ "<font>" | inlineCode }}</a> element (now deprecated) can be used to customise the font of its content. It has three attributes: {{ "face" | inlineCode }}, which customises the font family, {{ "color" | inlineCode }}, which can be a hexadecimal RGB colour or one of <a href="https://www.duncanritchie.co.uk/blog/colours-on-legacy-html-attributes">the many named colours,</a> and {{ "size" | inlineCode }}, which is a value from 1 (smallest) to 7 (largest). 3 is the default. You can also add a {{ "+" | inlineCode }} or {{ "-" | inlineCode }}, so that {{ "+2" | inlineCode }} in the below example means “2 sizes larger than the current font size.”</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<div align="center">
    <h1><font color="red">WARNING!</font></h1>
    <p>
        <font color="blue" face="Arial" size="2">
            This website is <br>
            <font color="purple" size="+2"><em>still under construction!</em></font>
        </font>
    </p>
</div>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<h2 id="creating-lists">Creating lists</h2>

<p id="def-ul-li">To create a bullet list, use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/ul">{{ "<ul>" | inlineCode }}</a> (“unordered list”) element. Each <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/li">{{ "<li>" | inlineCode }}</a> (“list item”) element inside is going to represent a single bullet point.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>This is my shopping list. It doesn’t need to be ordered, so I’m going to use an unordered list:
<ul>
    <li>eggs</li>
    <li>milk</li>
    <li>bread</li>
    <li>olive paté</li>
</ul>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p id="def-ol">A numbered list is created in much the same way, except you use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/ol">{{ "<ol>" | inlineCode }}</a> (“ordered list”) element. As with bullet lists, each list item is represented with the {{ "<li>" | inlineCode }} element.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>Let’s rank my favourite books:</p>
<ol>
    <li><em>Don Quixote</em> by Miguel de Cervantes.</li>
    <li><em>Moby Dick</em> by Nathaniel Hawthorne.</li>
    <li><em>Pale Fire</em> by Vladimir Nabokov.</li>
</ol>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>We can also change the first number of the list using the {{ "start" | inlineCode }} attribute:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>Now let’s rank my least favourite of the 9999 books I’ve read:</p>
<ol start="9997">
    <li><em>The Vindication of Eternity</em> by Jaromír Hladík</li>
    <li><em>The Concrete Garden</em> by Ian McEwan</li>
    <li><em>Milk and Honey</em> by Rupi Kaur</li>
</ol>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>An individual list item’s value can also be changed using the {{ "value" | inlineCode }} attribute:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<ol start="9998">
    <li><em>The Vindication of Eternity</em> by Jaromír Hladík</li>
    <li><em>The Concrete Garden</em> by Ian McEwan</li>
    <li value="1"><em>Milk and Honey</em> by Rupi Kaur (it’s so bad it loops around to being good)</li>
</ol>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>Sometimes you want the numbered list to use roman numerals instead. Or maybe capitalised letters of the Latin alphabet. These options are controlled by the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/ol#type">{{ "type" | inlineCode }} attribute.</a> Its possible values are {{ "1" | inlineCode }} for Arabic numberals (1, 2, 3...), {{ "i" | inlineCode }} and {{ "I" | inlineCode }} for lowercase and uppercase Roman numerals (i, ii, iii... and I, II, III... respectively) and {{ "a" | inlineCode }} and {{ "A" | inlineCode }} for lowercase and uppercase letters of the Latin alphabet (a, b, c... and A, B, C... respectively).</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<ol>
    <li>
        One thing we haven’t mentioned yet is that lists can be nested. This is useful when:
        <ol type="a">
            <li>You have more to say.</li>
            <li>You have more to say.</li>
            <li>You have more to say.</li>
        </ol>
    </li>
    <li>
        Another thing we should mention is that you can use multiple attributes at the same time.
        <ol type="A" start="4">
            <li>This allows you to continue the list we started above...</li>
            <li>...and add more things!</li>
        </ol>
    </li>
    <li type="i">
        Hopefully you’ll never use this, but you can also change the numbering of an individual list element!
    </li>
    <li type="I" value="99">
        All this is to say, <strong>HTML</strong> is really cool!
    </li>
</ol>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<h2 id="def-table">Tables</h2>

<p>A table is created with the appropriately named <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/table">{{ "<table>" | inlineCode }} element.</a> Each row is within a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/tr">{{ "<tr>" | inlineCode }}</a> (table row) element. Each row can consist of an arbitrary number of <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/td">{{ "<td>" | inlineCode }}’s</a> (table data, i.e. normal table cells) or <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/th">{{ "<th>" | inlineCode }}’s</a> (table header).</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<p>Our study yielded the following data:</p>

<table>
    <tr>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Age</th>
        <th>Tetris high score</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>John</td>
        <td>18</td>
        <td>50</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Anne</td>
        <td>21</td>
        <td>83</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Adele</td>
        <td>33</td>
        <td>120</td>
    </tr>
</table>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p id="def-table-caption">The above example works fine, but, especially for tables that are part of a document, it’s recommended to provide a <dfn>caption</dfn> using the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/caption">{{ "<caption>" | inlineCode }} element.</a> You can use the {{ "align" | inlineCode }} attribute to align the caption on the {{ '"left"' | inlineCode }}, {{ '"right"' | inlineCode }}, {{ '"top"' | inlineCode }} or {{ '"bottom"' | inlineCode }}. You can also use {{ 'rules="groups"' | inlineCode }} to show a border on the top and bottom of the table.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<table rules="groups">
    <caption align="bottom">Data revealed in our study</caption>
    <tr>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Age</th>
        <th>Tetris high score</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>John</td>
        <td>18</td>
        <td>50</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Anne</td>
        <td>21</td>
        <td>83</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Adele</td>
        <td>33</td>
        <td>120</td>
    </tr>
</table>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>In the olden days, before proper styling support, tables were often used to define page layout (as an example, see <a href="https://taras-shevchenko.storinka.org/poems-of-taras-shevchenko-translated-into-english-by-john-weir.html">this website on Shevchenko’s poems</a>). Nowadays, this style isn’t favoured anymore, but for beginners it can be a useful way to define headers, footers and the like, especially with the help of the {{ "colspan" | inlineCode }} and {{ "rowspan" | inlineCode }} attributes of {{ "<td>" | inlineCode }} and {{ "<th>" | inlineCode }}. {{ 'colspan="3"' | inlineCode }} means “this cell will take up 3 columns,” while {{ 'rowspan="2"' | inlineCode }} means “this cell will take up 2 rows.”</p>

<p id="def-big-small">You will notice we also use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/big">{{ "<big>" | inlineCode }}</a> and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/small">{{ "<small>" | inlineCode }} element</a> in the example below. They are self-explanatory ({{ "<big>" | inlineCode }} <big>makes what’s inside larger</big> and {{ "<small>" | inlineCode }} <small>makes it smaller</small>). We also use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/td#bgcolor">{{ "bgcolor" | inlineCode }} attribute</a> to change the background colour of the left sidebar and the {{ "valign" | inlineCode }} element to control the vertical alignment ({{ "top" | inlineCode }}, {{ "middle" | inlineCode }} or {{ "bottom" | inlineCode }}).</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<table>
    <tr>
        <!-- Left sidebar -->
        <td rowspan="3" valign="top" bgcolor="lightgrey">
            <a href="#">Home</a>
            <a href="#">Articles</a>
            <a href="#">About</a>
        </td>
        <!-- Header -->
        <th colspan="2"><big>My website’s logo</big></th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <!-- Main content -->
        <td>
            <h1>My article</h1>
            <p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet.</p>
        </td>
        <!-- Right sidebar -->
        <td rowspan="2" valign="top">
            <p>Related websites:</p>
            <a href="#">johnblow.com</a>
            <a href="#">picturesofcats.com</a>
            <a href="#">bayareanews.com</a>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <!-- Footer -->
        <td><small>This website was created by <a href="#">me</a> in A.D. 2026.</small></td>
    </tr>
</table>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<h2>Images and figures</h2>

<p id="def-img">So far, we’ve only talked about text, but a big part of the web’s appeal is the ability to include images. Images are created with the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/img">{{ "<img>" | inlineCode }} element.</a> This tag, like <a href="#def-br">{{ "<br>" | inlineCode }}</a>, is unpaired, so there’s no need for a closing {{ "</img>" | inlineCode }} tag. The source URL of the image is provided in the {{ "src" | inlineCode }} (source) attribute. You should get into the good habit of providing <dfn>alt text</dfn> using the {{ "alt" | inlineCode }} attribute. This text is displayed if the image URL is incorrect and is also used by screen readers to help visually-impaired users understand what the image represents. By default, the image is displayed in its actual size, which is usually too large; use the {{ "width" | inlineCode }} and {{ "height" | inlineCode }} attributes to control its size.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<img
    src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg/960px-%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg"
    alt="A drawing of a lion."
    width="350">
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

{% sidenote %}
<p><strong>Be careful about what images you put on your website!</strong> If they are copyrighted, you could get in trouble. The above image is from Wikimedia Commons and is licensed under the extremely permissive <a href="https://choosealicense.com/licenses/cc0-1.0/">Creative Commons Zero license.</a></p>
{% endsidenote %}

<p>Many websites like to take you to the image source when you click on it. This can be done by wrapping the {{ "<img>" | inlineCode }} element in an {{ "<a>" | inlineCode }} element:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<a href="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg/960px-%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg">
    <img
        src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg/960px-%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg"
        alt="A drawing of a lion."
        width="350">
</a>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p id="def-figure-caption">An out of context image isn’t too nice on your users. For this reason, HTML has the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/figure">{{ "<figure>" | inlineCode }} element.</a> By using it and specifying a caption using the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/figcaption">{{ "<figcaption>" | inlineCode }} element,</a> you can provide some additional context to the displayed image:</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<figure>
    <a href="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg/960px-%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg">
        <img
            src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg/960px-%22_A_Lion%22%2C_Folio_from_a_Dispersed_Nuzhatnama-i_%E2%80%98Ala%E2%80%99i_of_Shahmardan_ibn_Abi%E2%80%99l_Khayr_MET_DP240347.jpg"
            alt="A drawing of a lion.">
    </a>
    <figcaption>A Lion, Folio from a Dispersed Nuzhatnama-i ‘Ala’i of Shahmardan ibn Abi’l Khayr (MET, 13.160.8)</figcaption>
</figure>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>As you will have noticed, the {{ "<figure>" | inlineCode }} element has the added benefit of constricting the size of the image, so we don’t have to specify the {{ "width" | inlineCode }} attribute anymore!</p>

<h2 id="def-marquee">Leaning into the old web aesthetic</h2>

<p>As said at the beginning, this is a guide to creating simple websites in the old style. One unmissable aspects of the old web aesthetic are</p>

<marquee style="color:red" behavior="alternate"><span style="background-color:#9f9">marquees!</span></marquee>

<p>The <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/marquee">{{ "<marquee>" | inlineCode }} element</a> isn’t good practice in modern web design, but can still be charming when used sparingly.</p>

{% sidenote %}
<p>If you don’t do well with a lot of moving objects, you might want to <a href="#next-section">skip to the next section.</a></p>
{% endsidenote %}

{% htmlsnippet %}
<marquee>
    WARNING! Your Computer Caught A Virus!
</marquee>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>With attributes, you can control the direction ({{ 'direction="left"' | inlineCode }}, {{ '"right"' | inlineCode }}, {{ '"up"' | inlineCode }} or {{ '"down"' | inlineCode }}), behaviour ({{ 'behavior="scroll"' | inlineCode }}, {{ '"slide"' | inlineCode }} or {{ '"alternate"' | inlineCode }}) and speed.</p>

{% htmlsnippet %}
<marquee behavior="slide">Sliding marquee (will stop)</marquee>
<marquee behavior="scroll">Scrolling marquee (won’t stop)</marquee>
<marquee behavior="alternate">Alternating marquee (won’t stop)</marquee>

<!-- This should show a 2D bouncing DVD logo.
     It doesn’t work on all browsers. -->
<marquee behavior="alternate" direction="down" width="200" height="200">
    <marquee behavior="alternate">
        <em>DVD</em>
    </marquee>
</marquee>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

