<h1>A proper website</h1>

<p>Now, it’s finally time to use the above described tags and create an <em>actual</em>, presentable website. If we put the website content and save it as {{ "index.html" | inlineCode }}, it will certainly work. But such a website will have no metadata and isn’t very professional. We should adhere to the required HTML document structure. We’re going to need to use this basic structure:</p>

{% htmlsnippet "nopreview" %}
<!-- <!DOCTYPE html> tells the browser that the document is written in
    HTML5 and not any earlier version. Most browsers assume this anyway,
    but including it is still good practice. -->
<!DOCTYPE html>

<!-- <html> is the root tag of the whole document. The `lang` attribute
    specifies the language (en for English, de for German, fr for French
    etc. -->
<html lang="en">

<!-- The head contains the metadata of the webpage. -->
<head>
    <!-- Title of the webpage. You can’t use any tags inside. -->
    <title>My Website</title>

    <!-- This line makes your webpage look much better on phones. -->
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
</head>

<!-- The body contains the actual content of the webpage. -->
<body>
    <!-- Our page content goes here. -->
</body>

</html>
{% endhtmlsnippet %}

<p>All we need to do is to fill in the relevant blanks, write our content and look at the webpage we’ve just made in the browser! Here is an example to get you started:</p>

{% htmlsnippet "long" %}
<!DOCTYPE html>

<html>

<head>
    <title>My Website</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
</head>

<body>
    <table>
        <tr>
            <th bgcolor="black" colspan="2">
                <marquee><font color="red" size="7">MY WEBSITE</font></marquee>
            </th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td rowspan="2" valign="top" bgcolor="lightgray">
                <a href="#">Home</a>
                <a href="#">Blog</a>
                <a href="#">About</a>

                <h3>Table of Contents</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><a href="#frog-etymology">Etymology</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#frog-reproduction">Reproduction</a></li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td valign="top">
                <h1 align="center">Frog</h1>

                <figure>
                    <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red-eyed_Leaf_Frog_(49661076226).jpg">
                        <img width="330" alt="Red-eyed Leaf Frog" src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Red-eyed_Leaf_Frog_%2849661076226%29.jpg/330px-Red-eyed_Leaf_Frog_%2849661076226%29.jpg">
                    </a>
                    <figcaption>
                        <font size="2" face="Arial">
                            <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red-eyed_Leaf_Frog_(49661076226).jpg">Charlie Jackson</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0">CC BY 2.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons
                        </font>
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>

                <p>A frog is any member of a diverse and largely semiaquatic group of short-bodied, tailless amphibian vertebrates composing the order Anura. Frog species with rough skin texture due to wart-like parotoid glands tend to be called toads, but the distinction between frogs and toads is informal and purely cosmetic, and does not reflect taxonomy or evolutionary history.</p>

                <h2 id="frog-etymology">Etymology</h2>
                <p>The origins of the word <em>frog</em> are uncertain and debated. The word is first attested in Old English as <em>frogga</em>, but the usual Old English word for the frog was <em>frosc</em>, and it is agreed that the word frog is somehow related to this. Old English <em>frosc</em> remained in dialectal use in English as frosh and frosk into the nineteenth century, and is paralleled widely in other Germanic languages, with examples in the modern languages including German <em>Frosch</em>, Norwegian <em>frosk</em>, Icelandic <em>froskur</em>, and Dutch <em>(kik)vors</em>.</p>

                <h2 id="frog-reproduction">Reproduction</h2>
                <p>Two main types of reproduction occur in frogs, prolonged breeding and explosive breeding. In the former, adopted by the majority of species, adult frogs at certain times of year assemble at a pond, lake or stream to breed. In explosive breeders, the first male that finds a suitable breeding location, such as a temporary pool, calls loudly and other frogs of both sexes converge on the pool. Explosive breeders tend to call in unison creating a chorus that can be heard from far away.</p>
