<h1>Made to be shared</h1>

<p>This alone is a pretty fun way to spend an afternoon and you could theoretically send the {{ ".html" | inlineCode }} file to your friends and get them to open it in their browser, but you can actually put it online for free. There are multiple services that will host your website (this website is hosted on <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages">GitHub Pages</a>), but the most user-friendly one is <a href="https://neocities.org/">Neocities.</a> Sign up, press the red “Edit Site” button and upload your file, or write it directly in the browser! The whole point of the web is that it’s all in plain sight. If you want to check out the way someone else’s website works, right-click the web page and press “Inspect.” This will show you the page’s source code. Now hack around all you’d like; that’s the way you learn. On Safari, inspecting the site’s source code <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/40235028">is a little more complicated.</a></p>

<p>Big sites like Facebook, Twitter or Youtube rely on very sofisticated web applications running on a server that automatically generate a webpage for the given request. These web pages are called <dfn>dynamic</dfn>, since they are generated on the fly in order to reflect things like the number of likes, the number of subscribers (for Youtube), followers (for Twitter) or friends (for Facebook), the comments and replies, etc. In contrast, services like Neocities and Github Pages allow only <dfn>static web pages</dfn>: this means you don’t need to create a web application that would generate an HTML file on the fly (which would be fairly complicated, both for you as the user and for the service). Let’s say you name your Neocities website {{ "xyz" | inlineCode }}. When someone opens {{ "xyz.neocities.org/about.html" | inlineCode }} (your website’s URL), Neocities will see if there’s a file called {{ "about.html" | inlineCode }} on your website. If not, it will serve either a default “not found” web page or a custom one (on Neocities it’s called {{ "not_found.html" | inlineCode }}). If a folder is requested, the server will send the {{ "index.html" | inlineCode }} file. If you want your URLs to be cleaner, you can create a folder called {{ "about" | inlineCode }} and place the content of the “about” page in a file called {{ "about/index.html" | inlineCode }}. Now the URL of the “about” page is going to be {{ "xyz.neocities.org/about/" | inlineCode }}.</p>

<p>That’s all for now. There are many more things to be learned about HTML and about the rest of web design. You will notice that most websites (including this one) don’t really look like the websites we created here. That’s because as websites got more and more complex, there arose a need to distinguish between <dfn>style</dfn> and <dfn>semantics</dfn>. According to this distinction, modern HTML should communicate only the <em>structure</em> of the web page, not how it’s supposed to look. The <em>style</em> is left to a separate language, CSS (Cascading Style Sheets), which goes beyond the scope of this article. If you’d like to learn more about HTML, check out the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core">MDN Web Development tutorials</a> or the less serious <a href="https://neocities.org/tutorials">Neocities tutorials.</a> If you don’t remember what exactly an element <em>does</em>, it’s always a good idea to look it up on the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements">MDN Html Element Reference.</a></p>
